## Hardware (all verified working)

| Thing | Detail |
|---|---|
| Computer | Raspberry Pi 3B+, Raspberry Pi OS Lite 64-bit (Debian Trixie) |
| Kernel | 6.18.50+rpt-rpi-v8 |
| Sound board | ReSpeaker 2-Mics Pi HAT **v2.0** |
| Codec | TLV320AIC3104 at I2C address 0x18 |
| Overlay | `dtoverlay=respeaker-2mic-v2_0` (already installed) |
| Microphones | ALSA **card 1**, `seeed2micvoicec` |
| Speaker out | ALSA **card 0**, `bcm2835 Headphones` — the Pi's own 3.5 mm jack |
| Button | GPIO17, active low |
| LEDs | 3× APA102 RGB on the SPI bus |

### Why playback uses the Pi's jack, not the HAT

The HAT has a speaker socket but it is JST-PH 2.0 mm and the speakers we have are 1.25 mm — they physically don't fit. The HAT's amp is also only about 1 W, too quiet for a shop counter. So audio goes out the Pi's 3.5 mm jack into a powered speaker.

Record from card 1. Play to card 0. Both work at the same time.

### Confirmed working commands

Record 5 seconds:
```bash
arecord -D plughw:1,0 -f S16_LE -r 16000 -c 2 -d 5 test.wav
```

Play a file:
```bash
aplay -D plughw:0,0 test.wav
```

